## Proof

For each $i$, set $b_i = \sum_{j=1}^{mi} a_{ij}$. Since each $a_{ij}\in[0,1]$ and there are $mi$ terms, we have $0\le b_i\le mi$. The desired inequality becomes
$$\left(\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{b_i}{i}\right)^2 \le 2m\sum_{i=1}^n b_i,\qquad 0\le b_i\le mi.$$

Define $f(b_1,\dots,b_n)=\left(\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{b_i}{i}\right)^2 - 2m\sum_{i=1}^n b_i$ on the box $B=\prod_{i=1}^n[0,mi]$.

**Convexity.** The Hessian of $f$ is $2\,\mathbf c\,\mathbf c^{\mathsf T}$ where $\mathbf c=(1,\tfrac12,\dots,\tfrac1n)$, which is positive semidefinite. Hence $f$ is convex. A convex function on a compact convex polytope attains its maximum at a vertex. The vertices of $B$ are the $2^n$ points with $b_i\in\{0,mi\}$.

**Checking vertices.** At a vertex, let $I=\{i:b_i=mi\}$. Then
$$f=\left(\sum_{i\in I}\frac{mi}{i}\right)^2-2m\sum_{i\in I}mi=(m|I|)^2-2m^2\sum_{i\in I}i=m^2\!\left(|I|^2-2\sum_{i\in I}i\right).$$

We need $|I|^2\le 2\sum_{i\in I}i$. If $I=\varnothing$ this is $0\le0$. Otherwise set $k=|I|\ge1$; the elements of $I$ are $k$ distinct positive integers, so
$$\sum_{i\in I}i\;\ge\;1+2+\cdots+k=\frac{k(k+1)}{2},$$
giving $2\sum_{i\in I}i\ge k(k+1)\ge k^2=|I|^2$. Thus $f\le0$ at every vertex.

By convexity, $f\le0$ on all of $B$, i.e.,
$$\left(\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^{mi}\frac{a_{ij}}{i}\right)^2\le 2m\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^{mi}a_{ij}.$$

